          Greenworks sells more snow machines than almost any battery brand, spread across three voltages that do not share batteries. That breadth is the brand's strength and its trap: the right Greenworks is a very good value, and the wrong one is a machine on the wrong platform for your driveway. Start with the battery you already own, then match the machine to your snow.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Greenworks Snow Lineup at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Greenworks builds snow tools on three consumer battery platforms, 40V, 60V and 80V, plus an 82V commercial line and a handful of corded machines. The batteries are not interchangeable between voltages, so a 40V pack from your string trimmer will not run an 80V snow blower. That single fact should drive the whole decision, because a snow blower is the tool that eats the most battery, and buying into a second platform just for winter adds a second set of packs and chargers to the bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within each platform the machines fall into the same three classes every brand sells. There are power shovels, roughly 12 inches wide, for steps, decks and walkways. There are single-stage blowers, 20 to 22 inches wide, where a rubber-edged auger both picks up snow and throws it, and which clear right down to the pavement. And there is a 24-inch two-stage machine, where an auger feeds a separate impeller, for deeper snow and the plow berm at the end of the driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks changes kits and model numbers often. The same 80V 22-inch blower is sold with one battery, with two, with different amp-hour packs, and in retailer-exclusive versions with different listed throw distances. So this guide describes machines by platform and class rather than chasing every SKU, and quotes listed specs only where we found them on Greenworks or major retailer pages. Before buying, confirm the exact model number, the batteries in the box and the listed specs on the listing you are actually looking at.
          </p>

          <p>
            The short version is that the 80V platform is where Greenworks is most competitive for snow. Its 20-inch single-stage machine is the Greenworks pick on our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers</a> list, where we rank it as the best value battery option, and the 80V platform is the only consumer Greenworks line with the full range from shovel to two-stage. The 40V and 60V machines make sense mainly for people already invested in those batteries.
          </p>

